00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.280
One of the most frequent questions we get asked&nbsp;
on Journey to the Microcosmos is how James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.560
our master of microscopes, stays safe&nbsp;
while gathering and studying samples.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.720
After all, as beautiful as the microcosmos is,&nbsp;
there are plenty of parasites and pathogens in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.120
there that we’d prefer to keep under&nbsp;
the microscope and out of our bodies.&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.480
But while James has simple and effective&nbsp;
guidelines to protect his health—like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.680
making sure he always washes his hands before and&nbsp;
after handling samples, and never venturing into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.280
a pond with an open wound—there are times&nbsp;
when those measures don’t quite cut it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.000
These are the times when no matter how much&nbsp;
you protect yourself outside or inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.400
the microcosmos gets the best of you.
These are the times of the dust mite.&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.320
Now we don’t usually start these videos with&nbsp;
a warning, but we’re going to do that today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.880
While working on this video, at least 4&nbsp;
members of the Journey to the Microcosmos team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.000
found themselves having a bit of a&nbsp;
crisis about how clean their homes were.&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.640
If you’ve found yourself sneezing and wheezing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:44.720
running around miserable with a runny&nbsp;
nose because of dust, this arachnid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.200
is probably the culprit—though perhaps&nbsp;
not in the way you would expect.&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:57.440
Dust mites are small in the grand scheme of&nbsp;
things, but large in the microcosmos, hitting just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:03.920
around 300 micrometers in length. Their bodies are&nbsp;
decked out with four pairs of legs ending in feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:10.960
that act like suction cups as they wander in their&nbsp;
quest for three things: water, food, and darkness.&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.600
Dust mites love humidity because their&nbsp;
lives depend on it. They don’t drink water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:24.080
so much as they osmosis it into them, relying on&nbsp;
a salt-filled gland near their mouth to absorb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:30.160
water vapor from the air around them. So if their&nbsp;
surroundings become more arid, an adult dust mite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.400
will wither away until it eventually dies.
This is a challenge for dust mites that live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:43.520
in places with seasons, where wet and rainy falls&nbsp;
give way to dry winters. To survive those shifts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.480
they rely on the resilience of their&nbsp;
younger brethren—the nymphs—whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:54.320
forms are able to withstand the lack of moisture.
The seasons of the dust mite’s life was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.320 --> 00:03:00.560
important clues that helped scientists in the 20th&nbsp;
century understand their role in dust allergies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:07.440
Scientists identified dust as the source of some&nbsp;
kind of allergen in the 1920s, but they were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:12.880
sure what it was exactly about dust that did it.&nbsp;
They just knew that people all over the globe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.600
suffered from dust allergies, and that these&nbsp;
allergies were often seasonal, peaking in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.480
autumn, particularly after warm, humid summers2.
And other possible sources, like animal dander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:32.000
or mold, just didn’t quite fit right with the&nbsp;
seasonality of the allergy. But in the 1960s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:38.240
scientists in the Netherlands and Japan realized&nbsp;
that the dust mite might be the culprit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.080
They’re found in large numbers in the dust that&nbsp;
lines the unwashed and untouched corners of our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:50.720
homes, their populations peaking in time with the&nbsp;
runny noses and bleary eyes of dust allergies.&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.840
But while experiments confirmed that mites&nbsp;
really were the source of people’s sensitivities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.280
to house dust, it wasn’t clear what made them&nbsp;
so special. So, we’ll get to that in a bit...&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.920
James often finds dust mites in the humidity&nbsp;
chambers he uses to keep his microbes alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.680
These chambers are made up of a&nbsp;
dish lined with wet toilet paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:17.280
which is just about all a dust mite needs to feel&nbsp;
right at home—especially when there’s potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:23.040
some delicious microbes to munch on. So to keep&nbsp;
those other microbes from becoming a mite meal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.880
James has to regularly clean out&nbsp;
and disinfect the humidity chamber.&nbsp;

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:31.040
Now, that cleaning might get rid of the&nbsp;
dust mites in the humidity chamber, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.520
the problem is that our houses are full of&nbsp;
food for them because our houses are full of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:44.800
us—of flakes from our skin that shed and gather&nbsp;
all around and sustain the invisible mite.&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.200
And this is a problem not just because it&nbsp;
means the mite can thrive in our homes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:56.320
It’s a problem because food means poop. Over&nbsp;
the course of its life, a mite will produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:02.640
about 1000 pieces of poop that are roughly the&nbsp;
size of a grain of pollen. Inside those bits of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:09.040
fecal matter are enzymes that help the dust mite&nbsp;
eat its own poop and get nutrients that it might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.080
not have gotten the first time around.
But should the dust mite choose to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:20.480
revisit its prior meals, the feces will float&nbsp;
around the room, attached to other particles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:26.400
until eventually they settle down—perhaps on&nbsp;
a pillow, or on a pet’s bed, or a car seat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:33.200
It’s like we’re living in a gigantic snow globe,&nbsp;
except that the snow... is dust mite feces.&nbsp;

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:42.160
In 1981, researchers confirmed the bad news about&nbsp;
this animal’s poop— people are allergic to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.320
To be more specific, they confirmed&nbsp;
that dust mite poop contains specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.680
proteins that many people are allergic to.
So if you’re airing out some sheets and you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.040
sneezing, what you might actually be reacting&nbsp;
to is dust mite feces flying around the air.&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:02.800
Now, we apologize for this mental image but it&nbsp;
is reality, and it has embedded in our heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:08.640
so we have to share it with you as well. But&nbsp;
we can offer what might be a small comfort:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:12.160
there is another allergen from dust&nbsp;
mites that has nothing to do with poop.&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.440
Dust mites have a fairly lengthy mating process,&nbsp;
sometimes taking up to two days to finish—this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.920
is a pretty lengthy time for any organism, but&nbsp;
especially for an animal that’s only got about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:30.720
100 total days to live. Over the course of their&nbsp;
life, the female dust mite will lay up to 80 eggs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.200
which hatch into larvae that then go through&nbsp;
several different stages of development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.400
before becoming adults
With each passing stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.480
the dust mite sheds its exoskeleton, leaving&nbsp;
behind its youth. And that exoskeleton provides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.840
some of the other allergens for people react to.
But though that is something people are sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.240
allergic to, it really is mostly just the poop&nbsp;
that sets off people’s allergies. So I guess there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:04.240
really wasn’t that much comfort there after all.
And even for a trained and cautious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.080
microbiologist, dust mites can&nbsp;
become an unwelcome surprise.&nbsp;

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:13.520
Once, James brought home some samples taken from&nbsp;
a water dish that his neighbor left outside for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.240
their cat. The surface was covered in tiny round&nbsp;
things, and James thought they might be rotifers.&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.720
But when he looked at the surface of the scum&nbsp;
under the microscope, there were no charming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:30.240
rotifers to be found. It was mites, just&nbsp;
hundreds of them crawling around the slide.&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.440
It was so unpleasant that James&nbsp;
immediately bleached the slide.&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.880
Now, he’s not sure if that sample is the reason,&nbsp;
but for days after, James kept sneezing and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:45.600
having to deal with a runny nose, all of&nbsp;
which pointed to a potential mite invasion.&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:52.400
Fortunately though, our homes are not a utopia&nbsp;
for dust mites. What they really need is dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.960
which is why they prefer to dig themselves deep&nbsp;
into carpets and other soft things that give them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:02.160
space to burrow4. Dust mites have a harder time&nbsp;
with materials like suede that are difficult to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.200
hide themselves in5, and they usually avoid&nbsp;
hard surfaces that are exposed to light.&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:10.960
So for James, his weapons against&nbsp;
the mite invasion were clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:17.520
a vacuum, a mattress cleaner, and a strong&nbsp;
UV light bulb. It is one of the more ignoble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.800
ends to one of our organisms. We don’t like&nbsp;
to hurt them, unless they are hurting us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:31.200
But I am sure there’s a dust mite somewhere in our&nbsp;
homes right now, settling into a soft, dark abode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:39.840
of its own, with hardly a care in the world for&nbsp;
the battle that was waged to bring you this video.&nbsp;

